{"type": "result", "subtype": "success", "num_turns": 18, "total_cost_usd": 0.1325507, "result": "I created the XML data file and the final suite. All 8 tests in the suite pass.\n\n**Files**\n- `data/books.xml` holds 3 authors (`a1`–`a3`, with `country` and `born`) and 4 books. Each book has `id`, `author` (a reference to an author id) and `genre`, plus `title`, `year` and a `price` with a `currency` attribute.\n- `tests/xml/books.robot` is the final suite.\n\n**Step-by-step run**\nI used a REPL session and ran these one at a time, all of which worked:\n1. `Import Library XML`\n2. `Parse XML`\n3. `Element Should Exist` for author `a1`\n4. `Get Elements` for the books\n5. `Get Element Count` for the scifi books (2)\n6. `Element Text Should Be` for the author name\n7. `Element Attribute Should Be` for the price currency\n8. `Get Element Text` using a positional XPath, which returned \"Animal Farm\"\n\n**Suite contents**\n- The suite parses the file once in a suite setup and shares the root element across tests. The file path is built from `${CURDIR}`, so it runs from any working directory.\n- Tests cover the root element, author count, author name and attributes, book count, books by genre, book title and year, price currency and value, and a check that every book's `author` id matches an existing author.\n- I followed the project conventions: test names are Title Case and imperative, and each test has a `smoke` or `regression` tag.\n\nResults are in `results/` (`output.xml`, `log.html`, `report.html`). I didn't run `rf_analyze` separately.", "usage": {"input_tokens": 16, "output_tokens": 3969, "cache_read_input_tokens": 146767, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 19538}}
